We present a fundamental decomposition of certain functions defined on the sphere into an infinite series of Gegenbauer polynomials, leveraging the theory of special functions. We also establish a crucial criterion for positivity: a function is positively definite if and only if the leading coefficients of its Gegenbauer polynomial expansion are consistently positive. This key insight enables the determination of positive definite functions on the sphere using a straightforward test for the positivity of the coefficients. We demonstrate the relevance of this theory to zonal functions of functions on Lie groups, including specific examples such as radial functions of functions on Euclidean spaces and special cases in cosmology. This methodology becomes an effective tool for identifying positive definite functions in limited contexts. We showcase its application with two functions: radial function $\vert \mathbf{x} \mathbin{\circ} \mathbf{y} \vert^2$ and radial function $\vert \mathbf{x} \circ \mathbf{y} \vert^4$ of $SO(3)$ and provide a partial example of its application in radial functions of functions on $SO(n, \mathbb{C})$, particularly examining the case of $\vert \mathbf{x} \circ \mathbf{y} \vert^3$. Additionally, we include an appendix with supporting information.  All necessary background information is included in the text; consequently, no prior knowledge is assumed or required. \bigskip

